Our dynamic quantization framework aims to adapt to the dynamic workloads in LLM inference by decoupling the storage format of model weights and the computational precision.
The overall workflow of Kairos is shown in Figure \ref{fig:overall}.
Kairos takes a full precision LLM as input, and compresses it into a 4-bit model by using two-stage quantization (TSQ) algorithm.
Then, Kairos performs performance profiling for different computation path on the given hardware based on the weight shape of the model to obtains a hardware-aware lookup table (LUT).
We explain the details of this two part in Section \ref{subsec:TSQ} and Section \ref{subsec:profiler}.
The previous two parts are completed offline and only need to be done once.
Based on the quantized model and the performance LUT, Kairos constructs a precision converter for dynamically selecting the optimal computation path for real-time workloads.
We introduce the converter in Section \ref{subsec:converter}.